# Line endings are LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Everything this repository produces runs on Linux: the runners are bash, the
# images are Debian, the ConfigMaps carry those same scripts verbatim into a
# pod. A CRLF is not a formatting preference there, it is a syntax error that
# does not say so — the kernel reads `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and looks for an
# interpreter named "bash\r":
#
#     bash: /.../mermaid-render: No such file or directory
#
# naming a file that is plainly present and executable.
#
# Without this file, a checkout on Windows with the default core.autocrlf=true
# rewrites every script to CRLF on the way out. The committed blobs stay LF, so
# CI on Linux is green and nothing looks wrong; only the developer's own
# checkout is unrunnable, and the test suite fails there in ways that read like
# product bugs — a sourced library that silently defines no functions, and
# assertions that report an empty answer rather than a missing file.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes what a text
# file looks like once it has decided.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries git must never touch. Detection is good but not perfect, and a
# single "normalised" byte corrupts these silently.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
